  {
    "id": 19,
    "cluster": "Leadership and Strategy",
    "title": "Decision Making with AI",
    "description": "This competency defines the ability to integrate Artificial Intelligence into the executive decision-making process, moving from purely intuition-based leadership to AI-Augmented Intelligence. It requires the discernment to distinguish between decisions that are suitable for algorithmic optimization (e.g., inventory forecasting, resource allocation) and those demanding human judgment (e.g., ethical dilemmas, complex negotiations). Leaders must master the interpretation of probabilistic outputs, understanding that AI provides predictions based on correlations, not certainties based on causality. Crucially, this literacy involves recognizing and mitigating \"Automation Bias\" (the tendency to over-rely on computer suggestions) and maintaining strict Human-in-the-Loop accountability for high-stakes outcomes. The objective is to use AI to expand the cognitive scope of the leadership team—processing vast datasets to identify non-obvious options—while retaining the final moral and strategic authority.",
    "levels": {
      "Basic": "The leader uses AI to gather data and challenge their own assumptions but ultimately makes the call manually.\n\nSource Criticality: Consults AI summaries or data analysis to inform a decision but verifies the \"Source of Truth\" before acting (e.g., \"The AI says sales are down, let me check the raw CRM report\").\n\nBias Awareness: Recognizes that AI outputs can reflect historical biases in training data and actively questions if a recommendation reinforces a stereotype.\n\nOption Generation: Uses AI to brainstorm a \"Long List\" of potential solutions to a problem, expanding the decision space beyond the team's immediate ideas.\n\nOutcome: Makes more well-rounded decisions; reduces \"Groupthink\" by introducing an external (AI) perspective; avoids \"Analysis Paralysis\" by getting quick summaries.",
      "Advanced": "The leader uses AI to simulate complex futures and quantifies uncertainty before committing resources.\n\nSimulation: Uses AI to run \"What-If\" scenarios (e.g., \"Simulate the P&L impact if supply costs rise by 15% and demand drops by 5%\").\n\nConfidence Intervals: Interprets AI predictions not as facts but as ranges (e.g., \"The model predicts 80% confidence in this outcome\"), creating contingency plans for the other 20%.\n\nConstraint Optimization: Uses AI to solve multi-variable problems (e.g., \"Optimize the shift schedule for lowest cost while meeting these 5 HR constraints\").\n\nOutcome: Justifies strategic bets with robust data modeling; communicates risk more precisely (\"We have a high probability of X...\"); reduces the cost of failed experiments.",
      "Expert": "The leader designs the system of authority, deciding what decides (Human vs. Machine).\n\nDecision Delegation: Explicitly categorizes decisions into \"Type 1\" (Reversible/Low Risk $\\rightarrow$ Automate with AI) and \"Type 2\" (Irreversible/High Risk -> Human Decision).\n\nAlgorithmic Accountability: Establishes the \"Rule of Law\" for AI decisions—defining who is responsible if the algorithm makes a mistake (e.g., \"The Human Manager is always liable\").\n\nFeedback Loops: Designs systems where the outcome of every decision (Success/Failure) is fed back into the model, creating a \"Learning Organization.\"\n\nOutcome: Increases organizational velocity by automating routine choices; focuses senior leadership attention only on \"Edge Cases\"; ensures ethical compliance at scale."
    },
    "phases": []
  }
